mode: "100644"
text: "# Cadence & Capacity\n\nThe first decision in any content calendar is **how often** — and it's the one most people get\nwrong. This framework sizes cadence to reality so the calendar survives past week three.\n\n> Cadence norms below are general 2026 guidance — verify quarterly, and always match to capacity,\n> not to the platform's maximum.\n\n## Why calendars fail: overcommitting\n\nThe number-one reason content calendars die is **ambition outrunning capacity.** Someone plans\ndaily posts on five platforms, sustains it for two weeks, burns out, and stops entirely. A\nsustainable minimum you actually keep **beats** an ambitious plan you abandon — consistency is the\nasset, and you only get it from a cadence you can hold on a bad week, not a good one.\n\nSo: design for the **floor**, not the ceiling. What can you maintain when you're busy, sick, or\ntravelling? That's your real cadence. You can always post *more*; you can't un-break a streak.\n\n## Size cadence to real capacity\n\nEstimate honestly:\n\n- **Time** — hours/week actually available for content (creation + engagement, not just posting).\n- **People** — solo, or who does what (create / edit / schedule / engage).\n- **Budget** — tools, freelancers, editing, AI generation.\n- **Energy** — the realistic floor on a bad week, not the heroic peak.\n\nThen set a cadence that fits the floor. If the math doesn't work, **cut platforms or cut\nfrequency** — don't cut sleep.\n\n## Per-platform sustainable cadence (general norms — verify)\n\nDifferent platforms reward different rhythms; don't apply one number everywhere:\n\n| Platform | Rewarded cadence (norm) | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| TikTok | high — ~daily to several/week | volume + native; rewatch-driven |\n| Instagram (Reels/carousels) | ~3–5×/week | quality over volume |\n| LinkedIn | ~3–5×/week | dwell-driven; daily often dilutes quality |\n| X/Twitter | daily+ tolerated | higher volume is normal |\n| YouTube long | ~weekly | Shorts can be more frequent |\n| Pinterest | steady, ongoing pinning | evergreen, search-driven |\n| Newsletter | weekly/biweekly | consistency matters most |\n\nPick **one or two primary platforms** to do well rather than five badly — especially solo.\n\n## Repurposing multiplies output without multiplying work\n\nThe way to cover more platforms sustainably is **not** to create more from scratch — it's to\nrepurpose one core piece across platforms (a Reel → a TikTok → a LinkedIn clip → a carousel →\nX posts). One idea, many native cuts. This is what makes a multi-platform calendar feasible on\nlimited capacity. Route the actual transforms to `cross-platform-repurposing`.\n\n## Buffer and batching\n\n- **Batch creation** — make several pieces in one focused session rather than scrambling daily.\n  Cheaper context-switching, more consistent quality.\n- **Keep a buffer/queue** — stay a week (or more) ahead so one bad week doesn't break the streak.\n  The buffer is what makes the calendar *robust*, not just aspirational.\n\n## The minimum-viable-cadence rule\n\nWhen in doubt, start **below** what feels ambitious. A solo founder posting 3 strong LinkedIn posts\nand 2 TikToks a week — held for six months — will beat the person who planned daily-everything and\nquit in February. Add cadence only once the current one is comfortably sustained.\n"
